Abstract— The current technological trend indicates a convergence of augmented reality (AR) with heads-up display (HUD) in car navigation UI’s. In support of this trend, we propose use of small devices which have accelerometers or accelerometer-equipped smartphone as a basis of HUD interaction. In this paper, we defined the limited operation, experimented the basic operation and evaluated them. Then we obtained the good results.
I. INTRODUCTION
Modern car navigation systems help to convey a vehicle’s current location on the map, route guidance, points of interest (POI), etc. These systems can generally be divided into three types (See Figure 1):
1. The turn-by-turn type, with no display.
2. The embedded display type, installed in the dashboard or carried by the user. 3. The HUD type, which visualizes information on the vehicle’s windscreen.
Although the HUD type is the most expensive, and remains somewhat limited in both input and output, it has the distinct advantage of minimizing driver distraction. To support the further development of HUD-based car navigation, we propose a new kind of HUD system that integrates a smart mobile device with an accelerometer.
II. REQUIREMENTS
Our system assumes the existence of a vehicular HUD with
the following characteristics:
1. A simple user interface with intuitive operations 2. No-touch interaction (the user can only watch what is displayed on the windscreen)
To interact with the HUD, we proposed use of a mobile “smart” device equipped with touchscreen and accelerometer, communicating with navigation systems via Bluetooth or WiFi.
III. RELATED STUDIES
There are wireless mouse technologies. However, these interfaces are different from the smartphone, and users have to bring the mouse devices. Interfaces for wearable computing [1] have shown promise, but are ill-suited to driving, since drivers should not have to wear anything. Image processing technology with a camera is more suitable for this purpose, but requires intensive analysis of various positions under unpredictable lighting conditions. Accelerometer-based input systems [2] [3] are generally simple, but poorly suited to controlling the position of a pointing marker. However, since our target system involves a HUD, we can constrain the pointing range of our accelerometer input to the display area of the windscreen.
IV. PROPOSED METHOD
A. Overview
We base our system on two types of input operations:
1. Tap operations—indicating selection actions 2. Double-tap operations—indicating zooming actions
Given the limited information available on most HUDs, these two operations are sufficient for navigation interactions. To support such operations, we propose use of an accelerometer device for pointing. As with many mouse-based pointing interfaces, the color of the item beneath the pointer should be altered.
