3. Sensor device
The Vehicle information device is connected to the sensor device via Bluetooth. The sensor device sends raw input data from its 3-axis accelerometer. The vehicle information device processes this input and performs the appropriate operations.
V. BASIC EXPERIMENTATION AND EVALUATION
We performed an experiment using a sensor device mounted on the back of a glove (see Figure 3). Figures 4 and 5 show the accelerometer’s vector input for tap and double-tap operations, respectively. Note that the double-tap operations can be clearly distinguished from tap operations by counting the number of peaks (i.e. values above a given threshold) within fixed period. Figure 6 shows the input for long tap operations. Note that it is comparatively difficult to distinguish from the tap operation, given its similarity to the tap operation.
VI. CONCLUSION
In this paper, we proposed a user interface for HUD-based car navigation. Experiments showed that the distinctness of accelerometer input was sufficient for the limited operations of most HUD-based systems. In the future work, we plan to implement a fully functional car navigation system, and to test other factors including driver distraction, alternate pointing techniques, etc.
